course: religion-ethics
lesson: 28
title: Churches of the English-speaking world I
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Christians belong to different families of churches called denominations; they share the same core beliefs but have different traditions and ways of organising.
explain: The oldest split came in 1054, the Great Schism, when the churches of the East (Orthodox) and the West (Catholic) parted ways.
explain: In the 1500s reformers such as Martin Luther protested against problems in the Catholic Church, and the Protestant churches grew from this Reformation.
explain: Today in English-speaking countries you meet Catholics, Orthodox, Anglicans (the Church of England and the Episcopal Church), Methodists, Baptists and many more.
explain: For a child today it helps to know that a friend at a different church is still a fellow Christian: they agree on far more than they disagree on, and many now work and pray together.
fact: Denominations are different families of Christian churches with the same core beliefs but different traditions.
fact: The Catholic Church is the largest Christian church and is led by the Pope.
fact: Catholics celebrate Mass and have seven sacraments; the word catholic means universal.
fact: The Orthodox Church includes Greek, Russian, Ukrainian and other churches and is known for icons (painted holy pictures of Jesus and the saints, which people kiss) and chanted worship.
fact: For Orthodox Christians Easter is the greatest feast.
fact: In 1054 the churches of the East and the West split in what is called the Great Schism.
fact: In the 1500s reformers like Martin Luther protested against problems in the Catholic Church; this is the Reformation.
fact: Today Catholics and Protestants both admit faults from that time, and how to judge the Reformation is still discussed.
fact: Protestant churches stress the Bible, faith and grace, and include Lutherans, Anglicans, Methodists, Baptists and Presbyterians.
fact: The Church of England is led by bishops, with the Archbishop of Canterbury as senior bishop and the King as Supreme Governor.
fact: The Church of England is the established church of England and part of the worldwide Anglican Communion.
fact: The Episcopal Church is the Anglican church in the USA; episcopal means led by bishops.
fact: The Methodist Church was started by John and Charles Wesley in the 1700s; Charles wrote thousands of hymns.
fact: Baptist churches baptise believers by full immersion and let each congregation govern itself.
exercise: What is a denomination? | A family of churches with shared core beliefs but its own traditions | A building where only bishops are allowed to pray | A book of hymns sung in every church | A special name given to a baby at baptism
exercise: Which is the largest Christian church? | The Catholic Church | The Methodist Church | The Baptist churches | The Episcopal Church
exercise: Who leads the Catholic Church worldwide? | The Pope | The Archbishop of Canterbury | The King | John Wesley
exercise: What does the word catholic mean? | Universal | Ancient | Holy | Protesting
exercise: What is the Orthodox Church known for? | Icons and chanted worship | Silent meetings with no music | Brass bands and army uniforms | Baptising only grown-ups in rivers
exercise: Which feast is the greatest for Orthodox Christians? | Easter | Christmas | Epiphany | Harvest
exercise: What happened in the Great Schism of 1054? | The churches of the East and the West split | Martin Luther nailed up his protest | The Church of England was started | The Methodists left the Church of England
exercise: Who was a famous reformer in the 1500s? | Martin Luther | John Wesley | William Booth | Saint Patrick
exercise: What did the Protestant churches come from? | The Reformation of the 1500s | The Great Schism of 1054 | The first Pentecost in Jerusalem | The Salvation Army in London
exercise: Which of these is a Protestant church? | The Methodist Church | The Catholic Church | The Greek Orthodox Church | The Russian Orthodox Church
exercise: Who is the senior bishop of the Church of England? | The Archbishop of Canterbury | The Pope in Rome | The King | The Archbishop of New York
exercise: Who is the Supreme Governor of the Church of England? | The King | The Pope | The Prime Minister | The Archbishop of Canterbury
exercise: Why is the Church of England called established? | It is the official church of England | It was the first church ever built | It has the most members in the world | It was started by the Pope
exercise: What does episcopal mean? | Led by bishops | Led by elders | Led by the people | Led by the King
exercise: Who started the Methodist Church? | John and Charles Wesley | Martin Luther and John Calvin | William and Catherine Booth | Peter and Paul
exercise: How do Baptist churches baptise? | Believers, by full immersion | Babies, by pouring a little water | Everyone, with ashes on the forehead | Nobody, they have no baptism
exercise: How is a Baptist congregation governed? | Each congregation governs itself | The Pope in Rome chooses all of its leaders | The King appoints its pastor and its deacons | A bishop in Rome runs every Baptist church
test: Ruby says Catholics and Protestants aren't even Christians cos they're different. right? | No, they share the same core beliefs as Christians | Yes, only one church in the world can really be Christian | Yes, Protestants left Christianity in 1054 | No, but only Catholics believe in Jesus
test: wait so who's the boss of the Church of England, the Pope?? | No, it has its own bishops, with the King as Supreme Governor | Yes, the Pope has led it ever since the Reformation in the 1500s | Yes, but only on Sundays and feast days | No, it is led by the Methodist Conference
test: A classmate from a Greek family shows you a painted picture of a saint she kisses at church. What is it? | An icon used in Orthodox worship | A charm for good luck in exams | A Methodist hymn card | A Baptist certificate of baptism
test: My cousin in Ohio goes to the Episcopal Church. Which church at home is it most like? | The Church of England | The Catholic Church | The Baptist church | The Orthodox Church
test: my grandad reckons Martin Luther started the Orthodox Church. is he right | No, Luther's protest led to the Protestant churches | Yes, he founded it in 1054 when East and West split apart | Yes, he started it in Greece | No, he founded the Salvation Army
test: Jack says the Reformation was all the Catholics' fault, end of. What is a fairer answer? | Both sides today admit faults, and it is still discussed | Jack is right, only one side did anything wrong, so that is the end of it | It was all the Protestants' fault instead, because they were the ones who left | Nobody did anything wrong at all, it was just a friendly change of rules
test: A friend asks why her Methodist church sings so many hymns. Best reason? | Charles Wesley wrote thousands of hymns for it | Methodists are not allowed to pray aloud, so they sing instead | The Pope ordered them to sing in 1054 | Hymns replace the Bible in Methodist churches
test: Your Baptist friend was baptised at 13 in a big pool. Why not as a baby? | Baptists baptise people old enough to choose for themselves | Babies are not allowed in Baptist churches | Baptists think water is bad for babies | Baptists wait until people can swim well
test: Which split came first? | The Great Schism between East and West | The Reformation of Martin Luther | The start of the Methodist Church | The start of the Baptist churches
test: shut up, there's like a million churches, they all hate each other right | No, they agree on far more than they disagree on | Yes, every church thinks all the others are its enemies | Yes, they never pray or work together | No, there is really only one church anyway
test: The judge asks: "Why do you think the word catholic was chosen?" | It means universal, a church for everyone everywhere | It means old, because it is the first church | It means holy, because the Pope is holy | It means Roman, because it is in Rome
exam: Identify the event of 1054 whereby the churches of the East and of the West became separated. | The Great Schism | The Reformation | The Council of Nicaea | The Evangelical Revival
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the term episcopal. | It denotes governance by bishops | It denotes governance by elders | It denotes governance by the congregation | It denotes governance by the monarch
exam: Ascertain the persons to whom the foundation of Methodism is principally attributed. | John and Charles Wesley | Martin Luther and John Calvin | William and Catherine Booth | George Fox and Margaret Fell
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Baptist practice of baptism? | Believers are baptised by full immersion | Infants are baptised by the pouring of water | No baptism of any kind is practised | Baptism is conferred solely upon clergy
